.Dd November 1, 2026
.Dt PLAN 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm plan
.Nd manage daily plan files
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl -dir Ar directory
.Op Fl -show-path
.Op Ar selector
.Nm
.Op Fl -dir Ar directory
.Op Ar selector
.Fl m Ar message
.Nm
.Op Fl -dir Ar directory
.Cm ls
.Nm
.Op Fl -dir Ar directory
.Cm search
.Ar query ...
.Nm
.Cm init
.Ar directory
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a command-line tool for writing and managing daily plan files.
Each day is stored as a single plain-text file named
.Pa YYYY-MM-DD.plan
within a configured directory.
.Pp
Without
.Fl m ,
.Nm
opens the selected plan
.Pq today's by default
in an editor.
If today's file does not yet exist, a template is created containing a
date header and an empty inbox section.
When standard output is not a terminal, the plan is printed instead, so
.Ql plan yesterday | bat
works like a viewer.
Printing never creates a file, and exits with status 2 if the plan does
not exist.
.Pp
With
.Fl m Ar message ,
.Nm
logs
.Ar message
into the selected plan's inbox, stamped with the current time
as
.Ql HH:MM message .
Quote a message of several words.
A message of
.Ql -
is read as one line from standard input.
.Pp
The file template has the following structure:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
2026, Feb 20 - Friday
~~~~~~~~~inbox~~~~~~~~~
09:05 review pull requests
14:32 ship the thing
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

---
.Ed
.Pp
The line
.Ql ~~~~~~~~~inbox~~~~~~~~~
opens the inbox, and the first line of only tildes after it closes it.
Older versions sized these fences to the header line; such fences are
still recognised, and are rewritten to the fixed ones on the next write.
.Pp
The inbox is kept in time order: a new entry goes before the first entry
with a later time, and after any with the same time.
Lines without a leading time, such as notes typed by hand, keep their
place.
If the inbox fences are missing, they are reconstructed at the end of the
file.
.Sh COMMANDS
A first word of
.Cm ls ,
.Cm search ,
or
.Cm init
is a command; a message is only ever given with
.Fl m ,
so
.Ql plan -m ls
logs
.Dq ls .
A selector takes nothing after it; leftover words are rejected with a
suggestion to quote them.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm ls
List up to 30 recent plan files, showing the date, day of week, and
line count for each.
Files are listed in reverse chronological order.
.It Cm search Ar query ...
Search across all plan files for lines containing
.Ar query .
Matching is case-insensitive.
Output is in the format:
.Dl filename:line: content
.It Cm init Ar directory
Write
.Ar directory
to the configuration file, creating it if it does not exist.
.El
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl m , Fl -message Ar message
Log
.Ar message
into the selected plan's inbox, or today's if no selector is given.
If
.Ar message
is
.Ql - ,
one line is read from standard input.
.It Fl -dir Ar directory
Use
.Ar directory
instead of the configured plan directory for this invocation.
.It Fl -show-path
Print the selected plan file's path instead of opening it, creating
today's from the template if needed.
Cannot be combined with
.Fl m
or a command.
.It Fl -version
Print the version number and exit.
.It Fl -help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.El
.Sh SELECTORS
A
.Ar selector
names the plan file to act on:
.Bl -tag -width "N days ago" -compact
.It Ql @
Today.
.It Ql today
Today.
.It Ql yesterday
One day ago.
.It Ql @~N
.Ar N
days ago, where
.Ar N
is a non-negative integer.
.It Ql N days ago
.Ar N
days ago, in natural language.
.It Ql @-
The most recent existing plan file: today's if it exists, otherwise
the latest one before it.
.El
.Pp
When targeting a past date, the file must already exist;
.Nm
will not create new files for past dates.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width "XDG_CONFIG_HOME"
.It Ev PLAN_DIR
If set and non-empty, overrides the directory from the configuration
file.
This takes the highest precedence.
.It Ev VISUAL
Editor command used to open plan files.
Checked before
.Ev EDITOR .
The value is split using shell quoting rules, so flags may be included
.Pq e.g. Ql "vim -u NONE" .
.It Ev EDITOR
Fallback editor if
.Ev VISUAL
is not set.
.It Ev XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Base directory for the configuration file.
Defaults to
.Pa ~/.config
if unset.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/plan/config
Configuration file.
A simple key-value format with one supported key:
.Dl dir = ~/plan
On first run, if no configuration exists,
.Nm
prompts for the directory interactively and writes this file.
.It Pa YYYY-MM-DD.plan
Daily plan files stored in the configured directory.
.It Pa YYYY-MM-DD.<pid>.tmp
Temporary files used to replace plan files atomically.
A file left behind by an interrupted write is removed by the next write
to the same plan file.
.El
.Sh CONCURRENCY
Concurrent invocations are safe.
Writers take an exclusive advisory lock
.Pq Xr flock 2
on the plan directory, and printing takes a shared one, released before
any output is written.
Every write replaces the plan file with an atomic rename, so readers see
either the old or the new contents, never a partial file.
Log entries are stamped while the lock is held, so entries logged one
after another are never stamped out of order.
An editor session does not hold the lock.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 0
Success.
.It 1
Runtime error
.Pq e.g. I/O failure, missing editor .
.It 2
Usage error or, when printing, the requested plan does not exist.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Initialize a plan directory:
.Dl $ plan init ~/plan
.Pp
Open today's plan in the default editor:
.Dl $ plan
.Pp
Open yesterday's plan:
.Dl $ plan yesterday
.Pp
Log an entry into today's inbox:
.Dl $ plan -m \(dqreview pull requests\(dq
.Pp
Log an entry into yesterday's plan:
.Dl $ plan yesterday -m \(dqforgot to note this\(dq
.Pp
Log a line from another command into the most recent plan:
.Dl $ echo \(dqRemember to update deps\(dq | plan @- -m -
.Pp
Print the plan from three days ago:
.Dl $ plan @~3 | bat
.Pp
Search all plans for a keyword:
.Dl $ plan search deploy
.Pp
Print the path of the most recent plan file:
.Dl $ plan --show-path @-
